{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context\nA batch of 46 concurrent `search` calls was canceled at the client. A subsequent single `search` remained unserved and failed with `timed out awaiting tools/call after 300s`. The canceled calls were read-only.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Canceled or abandoned searches cannot monopolize MCP request capacity.\n- [ ] A later single tracker read completes within the normal request budget.\n- [ ] A regression test covers cancellation/backpressure for concurrent searches.\n\n## Root Cause\n\nThe MCP server offloads synchronous tool bodies through `anyio.to_thread.run_sync` with its default `abandon_on_cancel=False` behavior. When a client cancels a long-running tool call, the request coroutine keeps waiting for the worker thread and continues holding the default thread-limiter token until the synchronous body finishes. A burst of canceled long searches can therefore occupy every worker slot, leaving a later simple read queued behind abandoned work until the client-side `tools/call` deadline expires.\n\n## Plan\n\n- Add a deterministic regression test that constrains the AnyIO worker limiter to one token, cancels a blocked MCP tool call, and proves a later quick tool call can still obtain request capacity before the abandoned worker finishes.\n- Change the MCP sync-tool offload wrapper so client-side cancellation abandons the worker-thread result instead of pinning the request coroutine and worker-limiter capacity. The synchronous body may finish in the background, but it must no longer monopolize MCP request capacity after its caller is gone.\n- Keep certified-tool gauge instrumentation intact and do not add new store locks or throttle mechanisms.\n\n## Testing\n\n- RED: run the new targeted regression test before the implementation and confirm it fails on current `origin/main`.\n- GREEN: run `env PATH=\"$PWD/.venv/bin:$PATH\" pytest tests/unit/test_mcp_tool_offload.py -q`.\n- Run `env PATH=\"$PWD/.venv/bin:$PATH\" make lint` and `env PATH=\"$PWD/.venv/bin:$PATH\" make typecheck`.\n- Before push, run `env -u GIT_CONFIG_COUNT -u GIT_CONFIG_KEY_0 -u GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_0 -u GIT_CONFIG_KEY_1 -u GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_1 -u GIT_CONFIG_KEY_2 -u GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_2 PATH=\"$PWD/.venv/bin:$PATH\" make verify` and, if the known repo-isolation GIT_CONFIG artifact appears, confirm the named test solo with the same env-unset prefix.\n"}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788893184857677001,"uuid":"61203f77-b600-4b20-bf62-0057f93197ef"}